# Tutorial 19 — Saves, Rollback and Persistence
# Reference: docs/scripting/vns/runtime/vns-save-system.md
# Read scripts/tutorial/README.md for the complete course and linked references.
@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_19
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: There are three different timelines to think about: this session, a saved snapshot, and persistent progress shared across playthroughs.
@label lesson_menu
> Try a reversible story variable -> experiment
> Save and load from the player menus -> saves
> History and rollback -> history
> Persistent unlocks -> persistent
> Checkpoint -> checkpoint
> Finish and return to the hub -> return_hub
@label experiment
[set tutorial_token 10]
Lavender: We have ${tutorial_token} workshop tokens. Advance, then try the runtime's rollback action to revisit the earlier state.
[inc tutorial_token 5]
Lavender: Now we have ${tutorial_token}. The variable belongs to VN state, alongside dialogue position and character presentation.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label saves
Lavender: Run the full game to test its Save and Load menus. Choose a spare named slot, save with my expression visible, advance, then reload it.
Lavender: F5 and F9 are the default quick-save and quick-load shortcuts; bindings can be changed. A quick save overwrites the quick slot, so use the menus for a durable test checkpoint.
narrator: [save] performs a quick save. [quickload] restores the quick save. [menu save] and [menu load] open runtime slot menus.
Lavender: Test choices, scene changes, phone mutations, and your presentation settings after loading. Also test a fresh install and missing or old saves.
Lavender: The editor's embedded preview is not a substitute for the full runtime's disk and menu workflow.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label history
Lavender: History is the reading backlog. Rollback restores a previous story state. Re-reading a line and rewinding the simulation are different operations.
[history show]
Lavender: Close the backlog with its back control to continue. Inspect the previous token lines, then test rollback in the full game.
[history hide]
Lavender: External effects such as file writes or online calls need special care: rewinding dialogue cannot magically undo them. Keep those actions outside replayable story beats where possible.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label persistent
Lavender: Persistent variables are for things such as a gallery unlock or an ending the player has seen. They deliberately outlive a single save.
narrator: [persistent flag ending_lavender_seen]
narrator: [persistent load ending_lavender_seen]
Lavender: Those two lines are examples, not executed here. A tutorial should not silently change your game's permanent unlocks.
Lavender: Exercise: add a namespaced test unlock in a disposable project, restart the game, read it back, and clear only that test key. Never use a blanket reset in normal story flow.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label checkpoint
Lavender: Which state should remember that the player has ever reached an ending?
> A persistent unlock -> correct
> Only a temporary dialogue variable -> retry
@label retry
Lavender: A normal variable belongs to the current session or save. Use a persistent key for progress across playthroughs.
[jump checkpoint]
@label correct
Lavender: Yes. Keep current-route state in the save, and cross-playthrough achievements in persistent storage.
[jump return_hub]

@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_19]
[flag tutorial_done_19]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_4]
